Bluetooth: backport WCN6750 support so this tablet has Bluetooth at all

Without this the tablet has NO Bluetooth: Settings shows "No Bluetooth
found" because no hci device is ever created. Measured on gts7fewifi
2026-08-03:

    /sys/class/bluetooth/          empty
    hci_uart / btqca / hci_qca     not built at all
    btqca.h soc types known        QCA_ROME, QCA_WCN3990, QCA_WCN3991,
                                   QCA_WCN3998

The chip here is a WCN6750. Upstream gained support for it around Linux
6.0, roughly two years after this 5.4 vendor tree was forked, and Samsung
never backported it. So even a correct device-tree node cannot help: no
driver in this kernel can drive this part.

WHAT MADE THIS TRACTABLE (all verified before writing a line of code):

  - the tree HAS serdev: drivers/tty/serdev/{core.o,serdev-ttyport.o}
  - serial_core.c calls tty_port_register_device_attr_serdev(), so any
    uart_add_one_port() driver registers a serdev controller
  - msm_geni_serial.c does call uart_add_one_port()
  - the WCN6750 firmware is already on the device, shipped by the vendor
    firmware package: msbtfw11.mbn, msbtfw11.tlv, msnv11.bin

So the only real gap was the driver, and the gap is narrow: WCN6750 wants
the same modern WCN code paths as WCN399x, differing in the firmware file
names and in having its own regulator set.

THE APPROACH

qca_is_wcn399x() is deliberately widened to include QCA_WCN6750 rather
than adding a second predicate at all 13 call sites (11 in hci_qca.c, 2 in
btqca.c). Every one of those sites is asking "is this a modern WCN part
that uses the in-band-sleep/power-sequenced path?", and for WCN6750 the
answer is yes. The name is now slightly inaccurate; a comment says so.
Where WCN6750 genuinely differs - the firmware file names - it gets its own
branch, gated by the new qca_is_wcn6750().

Firmware naming, matching the files the vendor actually ships:
    rampatch   qca/msbtfw%02x.mbn      (vs crbtfw%02x.tlv on WCN399x)
    nvm        qca/msnv%02x.bin        (vs crnv%02x.bin)

VERIFY AFTER FLASHING

    dmesg | grep -i qca          # expect the serdev probe, firmware load
    ls /sys/class/bluetooth/     # expect hci0
    bluetoothctl show            # expect a controller

--- a/drivers/bluetooth/btqca.h
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/btqca.h
@@ -127,4 +127,5 @@
 	QCA_WCN3990,
 	QCA_WCN3991,
-	QCA_WCN3998,
+	QCA_WCN3998,
+	QCA_WCN6750,
 };
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/btqca.h
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/btqca.h
@@ -142,6 +142,19 @@
 static inline bool qca_is_wcn399x(enum qca_btsoc_type soc_type)
 {
-	return soc_type == QCA_WCN3990 || soc_type == QCA_WCN3991 ||
-	       soc_type == QCA_WCN3998;
-}
+	/*
+	 * DELIBERATELY includes QCA_WCN6750 despite the name. All 13 callers
+	 * of this predicate are asking "is this a modern WCN part that uses
+	 * the power-sequenced, in-band-sleep path?", and for WCN6750 that is
+	 * yes. Widening here beats adding a second test at every call site.
+	 * Where WCN6750 really differs - the firmware file names - use
+	 * qca_is_wcn6750() below.
+	 */
+	return soc_type == QCA_WCN3990 || soc_type == QCA_WCN3991 ||
+	       soc_type == QCA_WCN3998 || soc_type == QCA_WCN6750;
+}
+
+static inline bool qca_is_wcn6750(enum qca_btsoc_type soc_type)
+{
+	return soc_type == QCA_WCN6750;
+}
 #else
